The problem asks for the minimum number of operations to make a string of 'B's (black) and 'W's (white) entirely 'W's. An operation consists of choosing a substring of length $k$ and turning all characters in it to 'W'.

Let's analyze the operation. We want to cover all 'B's with intervals of length $k$. Since the operation turns cells to 'W', the order doesn't strictly matter for the final state, but we want to minimize the number of intervals. This is equivalent to covering all indices $i$ where $s[i] == 'B'$ with the minimum number of intervals of length $k$.

Let the indices of the black cells be $p_1, p_2, \dots, p_m$ in increasing order. We need to select a set of intervals $[l_j, r_j]$ such that $r_j - l_j + 1 = k$ and for every $i$, there exists some $j$ such that $p_i \in [l_j, r_j]$. We want to minimize the number of intervals.

This looks like a greedy problem. Let's consider the leftmost black cell that hasn't been covered yet. Suppose this is at index $i$. To cover this cell $i$, we must choose an interval of length $k$ that includes $i$. To maximize the utility of this interval for covering subsequent black cells, we should extend the interval as far to the right as possible.
So, if the first uncovered black cell is at index $i$, we should apply the operation on the interval $[i, i+k-1]$. This operation will cover all black cells in the range $[i, i+k-1]$.
After this operation, we look for the next black cell that is not covered (i.e., index $> i+k-1$) and repeat the process.

Let's trace this logic with an example.
